cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Smallest witnesses by girth for rad(n) | f(n)"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Ellena Godoy"
    given-names: "Jorge"
abstract: >-
  For a multiplicative function f, let S(f) be the set of integers n with
  rad(n) dividing f(n), and let the covering digraph of n have the primes
  dividing n as vertices with an edge q -> p whenever p divides f(q^e), where
  q^e is the exact power of q in n. This work computes the smallest member of
  S(f) of each girth, for f = sigma, sigma* and phi*. Any minimal witness of
  girth k has exactly k distinct prime factors and a covering digraph that is a
  pure directed cycle; its largest prime is bounded in terms of any known
  witness, which makes the enumeration finite; a per-arc strengthening of that
  bound makes the search several times cheaper; and a floor that mentions no
  witness at all, combined with doubling over an exhaustive search, removes the
  need for a known witness entirely. A surgery theorem inserts one vertex into
  the cycle of a witness of girth k to obtain a witness of girth k+1, which
  bounds the next smallest witness from above and, when the inserted stretch
  costs less than the exponent it saves, certifies that the next smallest witness
  is strictly smaller without computing it. All twenty-six published values are
  proved minimal rather than minimal among the primes examined, now including a
  fourth function, sigma**, the sum of the biunitary divisors. Twelve of them had
  not been computed before; sigma* at girth 9 had no known witness of its girth
  and so could not be reached by the earlier method, and sigma* at girth 10 and
  sigma** at girth 7 were reached only because surgery supplied an upper bound to
  start from. All results are verified by independent implementations and
  cross-checked against the count of prime-abundant numbers published by Pollack
  and Pomerance (2012).
keywords:
  - number theory
  - arithmetic functions
  - sum of divisors
  - prime-abundant numbers
  - covering digraph
  - girth
  - integer sequences
  - biunitary divisors
  - non-monotonicity
